        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of al-Qadisiyyah</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">November 636; the advance is widely believed to have begun on 16 November 636 and the fighting is told over four days, with a final day on which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rustam</a> was killed</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Al-Qadisiyyah, on the west bank of the river Ateeq, a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>; in 2024 researchers identified the likely site as an open field about 19 kilometres (12 miles) south of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> under Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, about 30,000 men including 7,000 cavalry, rising to 36,000 with the Syrian contingent and local Arab allies; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rustam</a> Farrokhzad, about 60,000 in one account, while modern estimates suggest about 30,000 on each side, though most scholars agree the Sasanians outnumbered the Muslims by a sizable margin</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> army won after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rustam</a> was killed and the Sasanian line collapsed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> was taken in March 637.</span></div>
