    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The fighting is told over four days, with a final day on which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rustam</a> Farrokhzad was killed, and the Sasanian line collapsed after his death.</li><li>Accounts of how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rustam</a> died differ: one is dismissed as unlikely for its literary devices and inconsistencies, and another is likely an invention of later story-tellers.</li><li>The defeat left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> unable to defend against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, which fell in March 637; the Sasanian Empire was annexed by 651.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 2024 researchers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durham-university">Durham University</a> and the University of al-Qadisiyyah used declassified American spy satellite imagery and texts describing the Darb Zubaydah, a pilgrimage road, to identify the likely battlefield.</p></aside>
    </section>

