    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">633</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a> wins a series of battles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and takes Al-Hirah</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">634</span><span class="rf-text">Abu Bakr dies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-al-khattab">Umar ibn al-Khattab</a> becomes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>; the Muslims are defeated at the Battle of the Bridge</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 636</span><span class="rf-text">The Sasanian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> armies camp at al-Qadisiyyah</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 636</span><span class="rf-text">The Byzantine army is defeated at the Battle of Yarmouk</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 636</span><span class="rf-text">The Sasanian army crosses the Ateeq and the first day of fighting begins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 636</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rustam</a> is killed, the Sasanian front collapses and the Muslims are victorious</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 637</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> is taken after a siege of two months</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-al-qadisiyyah" data-v="377aa63230" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>Both armies form up in four divisions</b> The Sasanian army crossed the canal before dawn and drew up in four divisions with the river at its back. The Rashidun army stood in four corps about 500 metres away, with Muslim cavalry at the rear of each.</li><li><b>Elephants and archers press the Muslim right</b> The Persian left wing attacked the Muslim right wing with heavy arrow fire, and the elephants led the charge. Jareer's cavalry was stopped by the Sasanian heavy cavalry, and the Muslim infantry began to fall back.</li><li><b>The Muslim right counterattacks</b> Reinforcing infantry from the right centre and a cavalry regiment under Al-Ash'ath joined the right wing. The infantry attacked from the front and the cavalry from the flank, and the Sasanian left wing retreated.</li><li><b>The Persian right and its elephants drive back the left</b> Rustam then sent his right centre and right wing forward after heavy arrow fire, with the elephants in the lead. The Muslim cavalry on the left, already in panic at the elephants, was driven back.</li><li><b>The elephants are turned back</b> Asim's archers shot the men on the elephants and his infantry cut the saddle girths. The elephants were withdrawn, the Persian right centre and right wing retreated, and the Muslims counterattacked.</li><li><b>Qa'qa leads the left centre forward</b> The elephants had been driven from the field on 18 November. On the fourth day the Muslim left centre surged forward and the other corps followed. By noon Qa'qa and his men had pierced the Sasanian centre but could not break the army.</li><li><b>The Sasanian front collapses</b> After a last stand the Sasanian front gave way. Part of the army retreated in order and the rest in panic towards the river, while Jalinus held the bridgehead and brought the bulk across. The Muslim cavalry pursued.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii.jpg" alt="Yazdegerd III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_the_Sasanian_king_Yazdegerd_III,_651_mint_(Cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group; [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After Muhammad's death, Abu Bakr established control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ridda-wars">Ridda Wars</a> and then launched campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> a volunteer army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a> won a series of battles in 633 and took the capital of Iraq, Al-Hirah, in the last week of May. Abu Bakr died in August 634 and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-al-khattab">Umar ibn al-Khattab</a>. Khalid was sent west with half his army, and the Muslim forces left in Iraq were too few to hold the region. Abu Ubaid, sent from Madinah, was defeated and killed at the Battle of the Bridge, and Muthanna withdrew the remnant, about 3,000 strong, across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> had been weakened by the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> of 602 to 628 and by a civil war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a> became emperor at the age of eight in 632, and real power lay with the generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rustam</a> Farrokhzad and Piruz Khosrow. In 635 he sought an alliance with the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> and ordered large armies to gather to reclaim Iraq. The plan was to be a coordinated attack, but when Heraclius began his offensive in May 636 Yazdegerd could not coordinate in time. Umar allegedly knew of the alliance and chose to finish the Byzantines first. The Byzantine army was defeated at Yarmouk in August 636, three months before al-Qadisiyyah.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad.jpg" alt="Rostam Farrokhzad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rostam Farrokhzad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rustam,_Yazdigird%27s_general,_slain_by_Sa%27d,_the_son_of_Vaqqas_the_Arab_-_The_New_York_Public_Library_Digital_Collections_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Darvîsh 'Abdî, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Umar raised new armies from throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and appointed Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, to command. Because of Sa'd's inexperience Umar told him to seek the advice of experienced commanders. The army was a volunteer force of newly recruited contingents, and Umar lifted the ban on the former apostate tribes taking part. Sa'd, who suffered from sciatica and boils, directed the battle from the old royal palace through his deputy Khalid ibn Urfuta. The infantry stood in four corps about 150 metres apart, each with a cavalry regiment at the rear. The army numbered about 30,000, including 7,000 cavalry, and rose to 36,000 with the Syrian contingent and local Arab allies. A force of 5,000 veterans of Yarmouk arrived on the second day.</p><p>The Sasanian army, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rustam</a>, was in camps on the east bank of the Ateeq, with a bridge as the only crossing. In one account it numbered about 60,000: 45,000 infantry in four divisions, 15,000 cavalry held as a reserve and about 33 elephants, eight with each division, on a front of about 4 kilometres. Hormuzan, Jalinus, Piruzan and Mihran held its wings, right centre and rear. Modern estimates, drawn from the logistics of the two sides, suggest about 30,000 on each side, but most scholars agree that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> and their allies outnumbered the Muslims by a sizable margin.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For three months the sides negotiated. Muslim emissaries invited Yazdegerd to convert or pay the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rustam</a>, inclined to avoid fighting, offered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> gifts to leave. The talks failed. On 16 November 636 Rustam had a canal converted into a road and the Sasanian army crossed it before dawn, facing southwest with the river behind it. Duels opened the battle. The Sasanian left wing attacked first, and elephants led the charge on both sides of the field, driving back the Muslim right and left. The Muslims answered with counterattacks, and on the left Asim ibn Amr had his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> shoot the men on the elephants while the infantry cut the saddle girths. The fighting ended at dusk, inconclusive, with heavy losses on both sides.</p><p>On 17 November the Syrian advance guard under al-Qa'qa ibn Amr arrived at noon, sending its men in small groups to suggest a larger force. There were no elephants that day, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> repulsed repeated attacks. On 18 November Rustam sent the elephant corps forward again and tried to seize the palace where Sa'd sat, but a Muslim cavalry contingent drove his regiment away. The Muslims blinded the elephants and cut off their trunks until they fled through the Sasanian ranks. The fighting went on through the night and on 19 November Qa'qa's men pierced the Sasanian centre.</p><p>On the final day, in a sandstorm, Rustam was killed. The Sasanians, not knowing of his death at first, fought on. Their front finally collapsed, and Jalinus took command of the remainder and got the bulk of the army across the bridge.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sa'd sent the cavalry in several directions after the fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>. Stragglers they met were killed or taken captive, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> suffered heavy casualties in the pursuit. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> gained large spoils, among them the jewel-encrusted royal standard, the Darafsh-i Kawiyan. Its jewel was cut up and sold in pieces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. Sa'd sent word of the victory to Umar.</p><p>The battle shook Sasanian rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> to its foundations but did not end it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, since as long as the Sasanians held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> they could try to recover what they had lost. Umar therefore ordered the Muslims to push on to the capital. The siege of Ctesiphon lasted two months and the city was taken in March 637, after which Muslim forces conquered the Persian provinces up to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzistan</a>. A drought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> in 638 and the plague of Imwas in 639 slowed the conquest. Umar is reported to have wished for a mountain of fire between the Muslims and the Persians.</p><p>The Sasanians continued the struggle to regain the lost land, and a Persian counterattack was repulsed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahavand">Nahavand</a> in December 641. Umar then planned a full-scale invasion of the Sasanian Empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a> was killed in 651, in the reign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Uthman, which ended the Sasanian line and empire.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/heraclius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heraclius.jpg" alt="Heraclius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heraclius_solidus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The course of the battle comes largely from the Muslim chronicles, which record several duels between champions of the two sides and give the days names. The chronicles tell the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rustam</a> in different ways. In one, Hilal ibn Ullafah cut the girdle of a load of weapons on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> behind which Rustam sheltered from the sandstorm, and the weapons broke his back before Hilal beheaded him; this has been dismissed as unlikely, because of suspicious literary devices and inconsistencies in the story. A version from Ya'qubi says that four men found the body. A third, in which Sa'd killed Rustam in single combat while Rustam was blinded by the sandstorm, is likely an invention of later story-tellers.</p><p>Other episodes are doubtful: the alliance between Yazdegerd and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> is only alleged, as is Rustam's rebuke of Yazdegerd over the basket of earth. The numbers are modern estimates from logistics, and most scholars hold that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> were the larger force. In 2024 scholars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durham-university">Durham University</a> and the University of al-Qadisiyyah identified the likely site, using declassified American spy satellite imagery and historical texts describing the Darb Zubaydah.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is considered one of the most significant engagements of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-persia">Muslim conquest of Persia</a> and, with it, of the early Muslim conquests as a whole. It left the Sasanian army unable to defend against the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> and forced it to retreat from all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. This opened the way to later offensives into the Persian mainland, including the battles of Jalula and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahavand">Nahavand</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> was annexed by 651.</p><p>The battle shook Sasanian rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and its memory was kept in the names of the days. The Muslim chronicles call the first day Yawm al-Armath, the Day of Disorder, the third day Yaum-ul-Amas and the night that followed the Night of Rumbling Noises.</p><p>The Arab fighters became known as Ahl al-Qadisiyyah, or Ahl al-Qawadis, and held the highest prestige among later Arab settlers in Iraq and in its garrison town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>. Umar, after the conquests that followed, wanted a break to manage the conquered territories and wished to leave the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, but later planned a full-scale invasion of the empire he regarded as his arch-rival.</p><p>The Persian view of the defeat was the opposite: one of great embarrassment, humiliation and scorn. The pride of the imperial Sasanians had been hurt by the Arab conquest of Iraq, and they sought to regain the lost territory.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At al-Qadisiyyah in November 636 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> army under Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas defeated the Sasanian army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rustam</a> Farrokhzad, who was killed in the fighting. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> fell in March 637, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> was annexed by 651.</em></p>

